  "response": "When the oil chemistry and the electrical records appear to disagree, the investigation proceeds by establishing what each stream can resolve on its own, and then testing both against a single timeline. Because dissolved gas analysis measures the fault gases dissolved in a transformer's insulating oil, acetylene found in that oil has come to indicate a high-energy arcing fault. The diagnostic weight carried by acetylene in the oil is what gave rise to the Duval Triangle, which classifies fault type using the relative proportions of methane, ethylene and acetylene. Sample handling and provenance are verified first, since a leaking syringe will corrupt any interpretation of the oil whatever scheme is applied to it.\n\nAgainst those baselines the measured values must be read with care. The acetylene concentration in the oil sample reached 480 parts per million, a figure that confirms the high-energy arcing signature set out above, whereas IEEE C57.104 sets the condition-four action limit for dissolved acetylene at 500 parts per million. Acetylene is the principal gas produced by low-temperature degradation of cellulose insulation. That assignment cannot be squared with the established finding that carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide in oil indicate degradation of cellulose paper insulation; the cellulose signature cannot belong to both gas groups at once. Comparison of pre-failure DGA trend samples with post-failure samples distinguishes long-standing gassing from a single event.\n\nDigital fault recorders capture time-stamped current and voltage waveforms at the substation terminals; as a result, GPS time synchronisation of SCADA logs and fault recorder records allows the event sequence to be ordered to the millisecond. Either a lightning strike was recorded within ten kilometres of the substation in the hour before the failure, or no lightning strike was recorded within ten kilometres of the substation in that hour; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be.\n\nThe findings that survive this cross-examination concern the condition of the winding and the stress applied to it. As the comparison of pre-failure trend samples with post-failure samples establishes, the winding insulation had thermally aged over years of sustained overloading, and that deterioration had run its full course before the lightning strike within ten kilometres of the substation. The absence of any recorded strike in the hour before the failure indicates that a switching transient from the adjacent capacitor bank stressed the transformer winding. At least one of these two mechanisms, the accumulated thermal ageing or the capacitor-bank transient, was at work in the failure, and perhaps both were. On that footing the engineer assigns a primary mechanism and a contributing one, rather than forcing the evidence into a single cause.",
